patches.behavior:
State per patch instance: `{ order: null, variant: undefined, warned: false }`. `order` is `null` (original order) or an array of source positions.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) { state.variant = variant; state.order = null; }
const items = ctx.inputItems("loop");
const n = items.length;
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.order = null;
if (ctx.pulsed("shuffle") && n >= 2) {
  const current = fit(state.order, n);
  let next = current;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 16 && sameOrder(next, current); attempt++) {
    next = fisherYates(n, () => ctx.services.random());
  }
  if (sameOrder(next, current)) { next = [...current]; [next[0], next[1]] = [next[1], next[0]]; }
  state.order = next;
}
ctx.output("output", loopOf(fit(state.order, n).map((p) => items[p])));
```

Helpers:
- `fit(order, n)`: `null` gives `[0, 1, …, n − 1]`. Otherwise keep the stored entries that are `< n`, in stored order, then append the missing positions in ascending order. `fit` never modifies `state.order`.
- `fisherYates(n, random)`: `a = [0 … n − 1]; for (i = n − 1; i ≥ 1; i--) { j = Math.floor(random() * (i + 1)); swap a[i], a[j]; }`.
- `sameOrder(a, b)`: same length and `a[i] === b[i]` for every i.
- `loopOf(array)`: emit the array as a Loop value.

- Pulses: `shuffle` and `reset` use `ctx.pulsed` (an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Consecutive-frame pulses each shuffle again.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then shuffle, so both in one frame gives a fresh shuffle.
- Guaranteed change: with 2 or more items, a shuffle never outputs the order it replaces (up to 16 draws, then a swap of the first two). With 0 or 1 item, Shuffle does nothing and stores nothing.
- Determinism: randomness comes only from `ctx.services.random()`, never `Math.random`.
- Live values: output reads the current Loop every frame through the stored positions, so updated or animating items keep their shuffled places.
- Retargeting: when the count changes, `fit` keeps the surviving items' shuffled order and puts new positions at the end in original order. If the count returns, the stored order applies again. Changing the patch's type resets to original order.
- Frame 0: original order. A Shuffle pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0, so the unshuffled order is never drawn.
- Loops: `loop` and `output` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame with one state per patch instance (and per component instance). Shuffle and Reset are single values read at loop index 0. If a loop with more than one item drives either, only item 0 counts, and the patch logs one warning per restart through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)` (`state.warned`).
- Edge cases: an empty loop outputs an empty loop.
- Muted: passes Loop through in original order; the stored order is kept for when it's unmuted.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the order back to original. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: same Loop and Shuffle ports; Origami's output is labeled Loop. Reset is Sonobe-only. Origami doesn't document uniformity, whether a shuffle can repeat the old order, or count changes; the rules above are Sonobe decisions.

State per patch instance: `{ inserts: [], variant: undefined, warned: false }`, where each insert is `{ at, value }`.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) { state.variant = variant; state.inserts = []; }
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.inserts = [];
const items = replay(ctx.inputItems("loop"), state.inserts);
if (ctx.pulsed("insert")) {
  if (items.length >= 10000) {
    warnOnce("Loop Insert reached 10,000 items, so this insert was skipped.");
  } else {
    const at = clampIndex(ctx.input("index"), items.length);
    const value = snapshot(ctx.input("value"));
    state.inserts.push({ at, value });
    items.splice(at, 0, value);
  }
}
ctx.output("output", loopOf(items));
ctx.output("outputIndex", loopOf(items.map((_, i) => i)));
```

Helpers:
- `replay(source, inserts)`: `items = [...source]; for (const op of inserts) items.splice(clampIndex(op.at, items.length), 0, op.value); return items;`
- `clampIndex(x, n)`: `Number.isFinite(x) ? Math.min(Math.max(Math.floor(x + 1e-6), 0), n) : 0`. The epsilon matches Option Picker, so 2.9999999999999996 counts as 3.
- `snapshot(v)`: a deep copy for JSON values; other runtime values are immutable and are stored as they are.
- `warnOnce(message)`: `ctx.services.log("warn", message)` at most once per restart.
- `loopOf(array)`: emit the array as a Loop value.

- Model: the patch stores its changes, not a copy of the list. Every frame it starts from the current Loop and re-applies the stored inserts in order. With a static Loop this accumulates, exactly like holding a list. When Loop changes (new data, or an upstream Loop Append added an item), earlier inserts are re-applied to the new loop. Implementations may cache `items` and replay only when Loop changes, as long as output matches `replay`.
- Pulses: `insert` and `reset` use `ctx.pulsed` (an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each insert, so a Repeating Pulse inserts on every tick.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then insert, so both in one frame gives Loop plus the one new item.
- Index: 0 inserts first; `count` or more appends at the end; negative or non-finite values insert first (they never count from the end). Number-to-index coercion at the port already rounds down and clamps at 0. The stored `at` is the clamped position and is clamped again on replay if Loop got shorter.
- Timing: the new item appears on the pulse frame. Value is read on that frame, after upstream patches have evaluated.
- Frame 0: no inserts, so output equals Loop. A pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: `loop`, `output`, and `outputIndex` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame with one state per patch instance (and per component instance). Value, Index, Insert, and Reset are single values read at loop index 0. If a loop with more than one item drives any of them, item 0 is used and one warning per restart is logged (`state.warned`).
- Retargeting: changing Value or Index affects only future inserts. Changing the patch's type clears the stored inserts.
- Edge cases: at 10,000 items further inserts are skipped with one warning, matching the engine's loop limit. An empty Loop with no inserts outputs empty loops.
- Muted: outputs Loop unchanged and Output Index `0 … count − 1`. Evaluation doesn't run, so pulses while muted are ignored; stored inserts return when unmuted.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears every stored insert. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: ports Loop, Value, Index, Insert → Loop, Index; outputs are keyed `output` and `outputIndex` here. Reset is Sonobe-only. Origami's tutorials show inserts accumulating while Loop is unconnected but don't show what happens when Loop changes; re-applying inserts is a Sonobe decision that matches Origami whenever Loop is static. Origami's default Index isn't documented; 0 matches the newest-first waveform in its audio tutorial.

State per patch instance: `{ appended: [], variant: undefined, warned: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) { state.variant = variant; state.appended = []; }
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.appended = [];
const items = [...ctx.inputItems("loop"), ...state.appended];
if (ctx.pulsed("append")) {
  if (items.length >= 10000) {
    warnOnce("Loop Append reached 10,000 items, so this append was skipped.");
  } else {
    const value = snapshot(ctx.input("value"));
    state.appended.push(value);
    items.push(value);
  }
}
ctx.output("output", loopOf(items));
ctx.output("index", loopOf(items.map((_, i) => i)));
```

Helpers:
- `snapshot(v)`: a deep copy for JSON values; other runtime values are immutable and are stored as they are.
- `warnOnce(message)`: `ctx.services.log("warn", message)` at most once per restart.
- `loopOf(array)`: emit the array as a Loop value.

- Model: the patch stores the values it appended, not a copy of the list. Every frame the output is the current Loop followed by the stored values. With a static Loop this accumulates; when Loop changes (new data arrives, an upstream patch edits it), the appended values stay at the end. This is the same model as Loop Insert with an index past the end.
- Pulses: `append` and `reset` use `ctx.pulsed` (an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each append.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then append, so both in one frame gives Loop plus the one new item.
- Timing: the new item appears on the pulse frame. Value is read on that frame, after upstream patches have evaluated.
- Frame 0: nothing appended, so output equals Loop. A pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: `loop`, `output`, and `index` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame with one state per patch instance (and per component instance). Value, Append, and Reset are single values read at loop index 0. If a loop with more than one item drives any of them, item 0 is used and one warning per restart is logged (`state.warned`).
- Chaining: a downstream Loop Remove or Loop Remove Last sees every appended item, so add-then-remove chains stay consistent. An upstream removal patch never sees items appended here.
- Retargeting: changing Value affects only future appends. Changing the patch's type clears the stored values.
- Edge cases: at 10,000 items further appends are skipped with one warning, matching the engine's loop limit.
- Muted: outputs Loop unchanged and Index `0 … count − 1`. Pulses while muted are ignored; stored values return when unmuted.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the stored values. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Loop Insert at End with ports Loop, Value, Insert at End → Loop, Index. Origami doesn't document what happens when Loop changes; keeping appended values at the end is a Sonobe decision that matches Origami whenever Loop is static. Reset is Sonobe-only.

State per patch instance: `{ removals: [], variant: undefined, warned: false }`, where each removal is a position.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) { state.variant = variant; state.removals = []; }
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.removals = [];
const items = replay(ctx.inputItems("loop"), state.removals);
if (ctx.pulsed("remove")) {
  const at = toPosition(ctx.input("index"));
  if (at >= 0 && at < items.length) {
    state.removals.push(at);
    items.splice(at, 1);
  }
}
ctx.output("output", loopOf(items));
ctx.output("outputIndex", loopOf(items.map((_, i) => i)));
```

Helpers:
- `replay(source, removals)`: `items = [...source]; for (const at of removals) if (at < items.length) items.splice(at, 1); return items;`
- `toPosition(x)`: `Number.isFinite(x) ? Math.floor(x + 1e-6) : -1`. The epsilon matches Option Picker, so 2.9999999999999996 counts as 3.
- `loopOf(array)`: emit the array as a Loop value.

- Model: the patch stores removed positions, not a copy of the list. Every frame it starts from the current Loop and re-applies the stored removals in order. With a static Loop this behaves like deleting from a held list. When Loop changes, the stored positions are removed from the new loop; a stored position that's past the end is skipped for that frame and applies again if the loop grows back. Implementations may cache and replay only when Loop changes, as long as output matches `replay`.
- Pulses: `remove` and `reset` use `ctx.pulsed` (an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each remove, re-reading Index each time.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then remove, so both in one frame gives Loop minus the one new removal.
- Index: removes only when `0 ≤ position < count` of the current result; otherwise the pulse does nothing and nothing is stored. Negative indices never count from the end, so an index-typed −1 (such as Option Equals with no match) is a no-op. Number-to-index coercion at the port rounds down and clamps at 0 before the patch sees it.
- Timing: the item disappears on the pulse frame. Index is read on that frame, after upstream patches have evaluated, so a same-frame Loop Option Switch result is used.
- Frame 0: no removals, so output equals Loop. A pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: `loop`, `output`, and `outputIndex` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame with one state per patch instance (and per component instance). Index, Remove, and Reset are single values read at loop index 0. If a loop with more than one item drives any of them, item 0 is used and one warning per restart is logged through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)` (`state.warned`).
- Chaining: positions refer to this patch's own result. Put removal patches after patches that add items (Loop Append, Loop Insert), and show the last patch's output.
- Retargeting: changing Index affects only future removals. Changing the patch's type clears the stored removals.
- Edge cases: removing from an empty loop does nothing.
- Muted: outputs Loop unchanged and Output Index `0 … count − 1`. Pulses while muted are ignored; stored removals return when unmuted.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the stored removals. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: ports Loop, Index, Remove → Loop, Index; outputs are keyed `output` and `outputIndex` here. Origami's identifier suffix is `delete` (its `remove` suffix is Loop Remove Last). Origami doesn't document out-of-range Index or what happens when Loop changes; the no-op and re-applied removals are Sonobe decisions that match Origami whenever Loop is static. Reset is Sonobe-only.

State per patch instance: `{ removed: 0, variant: undefined, warned: false }`.

Each frame:
```
const variant = ctx.typeParam ?? "number"; // undefined typeParam means the first variant
if (state.variant !== variant) { state.variant = variant; state.removed = 0; }
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.removed = 0;
const source = ctx.inputItems("loop");
let items = source.slice(0, Math.max(0, source.length - state.removed));
if (ctx.pulsed("removeLast") && items.length > 0) {
  state.removed += 1;
  items = items.slice(0, -1);
}
ctx.output("output", loopOf(items));
ctx.output("index", loopOf(items.map((_, i) => i)));
```

`loopOf(array)` means "emit this array as a Loop value".

- Model: the patch stores a count of removed items, not a copy of the list. Every frame the output is the current Loop without its last `removed` items. With a static Loop this behaves like popping from a held list. When Loop grows (an upstream Loop Append or Loop Insert added an item), the same number of items still comes off the end, so an insert-then-remove-last chain keeps a constant length.
- Pulses: `removeLast` and `reset` use `ctx.pulsed` (an upstream pulse or a false→true edge of a connected boolean). Pulses on consecutive frames each remove one more item.
- Precedence: reset runs first, then removeLast, so both in one frame gives Loop minus its last item.
- Empty lists: a pulse when the current result is empty does nothing and isn't counted, so it never removes items that arrive later.
- Shrinking input: if Loop gets shorter than `removed`, the output is empty; if it grows back, the stored count applies again.
- Timing: the item disappears on the pulse frame.
- Frame 0: nothing removed, so output equals Loop. A pulse on frame 0 applies on frame 0.
- Loops: `loop`, `output`, and `index` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame with one state per patch instance (and per component instance). Remove Last and Reset are single values read at loop index 0. If a loop with more than one item drives either, item 0 is used and one warning per restart is logged through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)` (`state.warned`).
- Chaining: put this patch after patches that add items, and show its output.
- Retargeting: changing the patch's type clears the count.
- Muted: outputs Loop unchanged and Index `0 … count − 1`. Pulses while muted are ignored; the stored count returns when unmuted.
- Restart and dispose: restart sets the count back to 0. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: ports Loop, Remove Last → Loop, Index. Origami's identifier suffix is `remove` (its `delete` suffix is Loop Remove), and its Loop output description says "with the last value" where it means without. Origami doesn't document empty lists or changing input; the rules above are Sonobe decisions that match Origami whenever Loop is static. Reset is Sonobe-only.

Stateless (apart from one-time warning flags).

Each frame:
```
const sources = ctx.inputItems("array"); // a single JSON value arrives as a 1-item array; a Loop as its items
const items = [];
for (const source of sources) {
  if (Array.isArray(source)) items.push(...source);
  else if (source === null || source === undefined) continue;
  else items.push(source);
}
if (sources.length === 1) warnIfSuspicious(sources[0]);
ctx.output("items", loopOf(items));
ctx.output("index", loopOf(items.map((_, i) => i)));
```

`loopOf(array)` means "emit this array as a Loop value".

- Elements: an array contributes its elements in order. Nested arrays aren't flattened further; an element that's an array stays one item. Items are the JSON values themselves (never copied or re-typed); coercion into typed ports happens at the receiving port (json → any, best effort, core `values.ts`).
- Non-array input: `null` (for example, a Network Request that hasn't finished) gives empty loops. Any other single value (object, number, text, boolean) gives a one-item loop holding it.
- `warnIfSuspicious(v)`: logs at most one `ctx.services.log("warn", …)` per restart when a single, non-looped Array is an object ("Loop Over Array got an object, not an array. Use Value for Key to pick the array inside it.") or text whose first non-space character is `[` ("Loop Over Array got text that looks like JSON. Convert it with Text to JSON first.").
- Loop input: when a loop drives Array, each loop item is treated as above and the results are joined in loop order (flattened one level), like looped component outputs. Index counts across the joined result.
- Frame 0: evaluates with authored values. Before data arrives the result is empty, so bound layers render 0 instances.
- Loops: `array`, `items`, and `index` are whole-loop ports; the patch evaluates once per frame. The output count follows the array's length every frame.
- Retargeting: a new array (such as a finished request) replaces the loop on the same frame; per-index state in downstream patches follows the engine's grow and shrink rules.
- Edge cases: `[]` gives empty loops. The engine's 10,000-item loop limit applies to long arrays.
- Muted: both outputs are empty loops.
- Restart and dispose: nothing to reset or release; warning flags clear on restart.
- Coming from Origami: same ports (Array → Index, Items), with Items listed first here. Origami doesn't document non-array input or a looped Array; the rules above are Sonobe decisions. Files store the identifier as `builtin.loop.fromArray`.
